+        <article class="journal-text" id="journal-text">
+          <p>Every bag of coffee that leaves our roastery starts with a relationship. Long before a single bean
+            hits the drum, our green coffee team spends months each year traveling to the farms and washing
+            stations we work with in Ethiopia, Colombia, Guatemala, and Sumatra. We walk the rows with
+            producers, taste cherries straight off the branch, and talk through what the coming harvest looks
+            like. These visits are not a formality; they are the foundation of everything we do.</p>
+
+          <h3>Direct Trade, Not Just a Label</h3>
+          <p>We buy the majority of our coffee through direct trade agreements, which means we negotiate
+            prices face to face with the farmers and cooperatives who grow it. On average, we pay between
+            two and three times the commodity market price, and we commit to multi-year purchasing contracts
+            so that producers can plan ahead, invest in their land, and weather the inevitable bad seasons.
+            When a frost or a dry spell hits, we do not walk away. Several of our partner farms have been
+            with us for more than a decade.</p>
+
+          <h3>Cupping for Quality</h3>
+          <p>Once samples arrive at our roastery, they go through a rigorous cupping process. Our team
+            evaluates each lot blind, scoring aroma, acidity, body, sweetness, and finish according to
+            specialty coffee standards. Only coffees that score 85 points or higher make it onto our menu.
+            We also roast small test batches at several different profiles to find the one that best
+            expresses the character of the bean, whether that is the bright blueberry notes of a natural
+            Ethiopian or the chocolatey depth of a washed Colombian.</p>
+
+          <h3>Caring for the Land</h3>
+          <p>Sustainability is woven into our sourcing decisions. We prioritize farms that grow coffee under
+            native shade canopy, which protects biodiversity, provides habitat for migratory birds, and
+            naturally enriches the soil. We help fund water treatment projects at washing stations so that
+            the runoff from processing does not pollute local rivers, and we share the cost of organic
+            certification with smaller growers who could not otherwise afford it.</p>
+
+          <h3>Roasted Fresh, Close to Home</h3>
+          <p>Finally, we roast in small batches right here in town, never more than a week before the coffee
+            reaches your cup. Every bag is stamped with its roast date and the name of the farm it came from,
+            because we believe you deserve to know exactly who grew your coffee. When you take a sip of your
+            morning latte, you are tasting the work of hundreds of hands, and we are proud to be one small
+            link in that chain.</p>
